(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-microbe-human-interactions-quiz"]={"title":"Microbe-Human Interactions — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which adhesion mechanism does the lecture attribute to viruses?","choices":["Suction disks","Hooks and barbs","Adhesive slimes and capsules","Spike or capsid proteins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the term sequelae mean, per the lecture?","choices":["The period before symptoms appear","The route by which a pathogen leaves","A second infection complicating the first","Long-term or permanent tissue or organ damage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — paralysis from polio, deafness from meningitis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What proportion of the human genome does the lecture describe as foreign in origin?","choices":["About nine tenths","About half","About a tenth","Almost none"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — viral remnants and transposons. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What defines a focal infection?","choices":["An agent breaks loose from a local site and spreads","Several microbes grow at one site","An infection confined to one tissue","A second infection following disruption of the flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient develops bacterial pneumonia on top of a viral upper respiratory infection. Which term applies?","choices":["Secondary infection","Superinfection","Mixed infection","Focal infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a different microbe complicating the initial one. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which is given as an example of a systemic infection?","choices":["Antibiotic-associated colitis","Measles","A boil","A wart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with chicken pox, anthrax and rabies. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which organisms are the most common oral residents?","choices":["Coliforms","Aerobic streptococci","Strict anaerobes","Staphylococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which categories of toxin does the lecture name under toxigenicity?","choices":["Neurotoxins, enterotoxins, hemotoxins, nephrotoxins","Exoenzymes, leukocidins and antiphagocytic factors","Capsules and slimes","Fimbriae and flagella"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, grouped by the tissue targeted. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the key difference in how endo- and exotoxins are released?","choices":["Endotoxin is secreted; exotoxin released on lysis","Endotoxin on lysis; exotoxin secreted by living cells","Both are secreted continuously","Both require host cell death first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish a sign from a symptom?","choices":["A sign is observed; a symptom is felt by the patient","A sign is felt; a symptom is observed","A sign is early; a symptom is late","A sign is specific; a symptom is non-specific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a sign is often measurable by the clinician. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which host factor does the lecture single out with particular emphasis?","choices":["Height and weight","Geographic location","Immune status","Blood group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture calls it the major one. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which situations allow an opportunistic pathogen to cause disease?","choices":["Only after antibiotic therapy","Only in hospital settings","Compromised defences, or growth in an unnatural site","Any exposure at all, regardless of the host's status"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and sometimes it is the patient's own flora attacking. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What consequence of nosocomial infection does the lecture emphasise?","choices":["It resolves without treatment","It may prolong the hospital stay or end in death","It always requires surgical drainage of the site","It is usually asymptomatic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which pattern describes several microbes growing at one site simultaneously?","choices":["Focal infection","Systemic infection","Mixed infection","Secondary infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which term describes microbes that remain confined to one tissue?","choices":["Localized infection","Systemic infection","Focal infection","Mixed infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — boils and warts are the examples. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which sites does the lecture say nosocomial infections most commonly involve?","choices":["Urinary tract, respiratory tract and surgical incisions","Skin and soft tissue only","Bloodstream and central nervous system infections","Gastrointestinal tract alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the ratio of non-human to human cells in the body?","choices":["They are exactly equal","Non-human cells are a tiny fraction","Non-human cells slightly outnumber human ones","Human cells outnumber non-human ten to one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: roughly 38 to 39 trillion against about 30 trillion. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which structure does the lecture list as a bacterial adhesion mechanism?","choices":["Ribosomes","Plasmids","Endospores","Fimbriae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, also called attachment pili. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which of these does the lecture list as normally sterile?","choices":["The lumen of the large intestine","The skin surface and hair follicles","The oral cavity and pharynx","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside kidneys, bladder, brain and the interior of the eye. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where does the lecture locate bacterial endotoxin?","choices":["The flagellum","Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide","A protein secreted by living cells","The bacterial capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, released from lysed or damaged gram-negative bacteria. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the function of a leukocidin?","choices":["Destroying leukocytes","Dissolving connective tissue","Clotting plasma","Blocking antibody binding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the suffix means to kill. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which is an example of vertical transmission?","choices":["A mosquito bite","Contaminated food","Mother to child at birth","Kissing or sexual contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, transplacental or during vaginal birth. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"The lecture notes that disease may be caused by factors other than the microbe itself. Which does it name?","choices":["The portal of exit","Our own immune responses","Antibiotic resistance genes","The infectious dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; disease may follow from the host response as much as the organism. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is a reportable, or notifiable, disease?","choices":["One with a high mortality rate in the population","One that is always epidemic","One requiring hospital admission","One that must be reported to public health authorities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which body publishes the Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report?","choices":["The Food and Drug Administration","The National Institutes of Health","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The World Health Organization in Geneva"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; the World Health Organization is the international counterpart. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does the lecture say resident microflora benefit the host?","choices":["By preventing overgrowth of harmful microbes","By producing all essential vitamins","By neutralising bacterial toxins","By stimulating antibody production directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — competitive exclusion. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the urinary tract in females?","choices":["Colonised only after catheterisation","Sterile except a short portion of the urethra","Sterile throughout, including the urethra","Colonised throughout by lactobacilli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the same is true in males. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which organisms can cause zoonotic infection, per the lecture?","choices":["Only viruses and bacteria","Only bacterial and fungal agents","Only parasitic protozoa","Bacterial, viral, fungal or protozoan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the category is defined by the reservoir, not the organism. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does prevalence measure?","choices":["New cases over a defined time period","Deaths per hundred thousand","Cases per hundred thousand","Total existing cases in the population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, usually as a percentage. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does incidence measure?","choices":["Deaths attributable to a disease","The proportion who recover","New cases over a certain time period","All existing cases at one moment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, against the general healthy population. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 53"}]};
